## Lab / Hands-On Work

<!-- Describe what you did in the lab. Include steps, commands, or screenshots descriptions -->

### What I did
I follow up with the steps used by the trainer in creating RBAC polcy
1. Define governance requirements
2. Create or choose Azure Policy definitions
3. Assign the policy or initiative

Assign policies at the appropriate scope:
Management Group
Subscription
Resource Group
Individual Resource
Configure parameters and exclusions if needed
4. Select the policy effect
Common policy effects include:

Audit – Reports non-compliant resources.
Deny – Prevents deployment of non-compliant resources.
Append – Adds required properties.
Modify – Automatically updates resource properties.
DeployIfNotExists (DINE) – Deploys required resources when missing.
AuditIfNotExists – Audits resources missing required configurations.
Disabled – Turns off policy enforcement.
5. Evaluate compliance

Azure automatically evaluates resources against assigned policies.
Review compliance status to identify non-compliant resources.
6.Remediate non-compliant resources

Create remediation tasks for policies that support Modify or DeployIfNotExists.
Automatically bring existing resources into compliance where possible
7. Monitor and report

Monitor compliance using Azure Policy dashboards.
Integrate with Azure Monitor, Log Analytics, or Microsoft Defender for Cloud for reporting and alerts.



